  "theory": {
"intro": "For an integer $n \\ge 1$, the **factorial** $n!$ is the product $n \\times (n-1) \\times (n-2) \\times \\dots \\times 2 \\times 1$. By definition $0! = 1$. For integers $n \\ge 1$, each factorial also **unrolls** as $n! = n \\times (n-1)!$, which is how factorial expressions are simplified.",
"facts": [
"**Factorial notation**: for a whole number $n \\ge 1$, $n! = n \\times (n-1) \\times (n-2) \\times \\dots \\times 2 \\times 1$.",
"**Zero factorial**: $0! = 1$, not $0$.",
"**Recursive relation**: $n! = n \\times (n-1)!$ for integers $n \\ge 1$, and unrolling again gives $n! = n(n-1)(n-2)!$ for integers $n \\ge 2$.",
"In this skill, factorials are defined only for whole numbers, so $1.5!$ and $(-3)!$ are undefined. Factorial arguments in algebraic expressions must be non-negative integers.",
"To simplify a quotient, unroll the larger factorial until the smaller one appears, then cancel: $\\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!} = n(n-1)\\dots(n-r+1)$ for integers $0 \\le r \\le n$. When $r = 0$, the product has no factors and equals $1$.",
"To simplify a sum or difference, take out the smallest factorial: $(n+1)! - n! = n!(n+1-1) = n \\cdot n!$.",
"To combine fractions, unroll the larger factorial in the denominator to get a common denominator."
],
"steps": [
          "Identify the factorials in the expression and determine the largest and smallest factorial terms.",
          "Unroll the larger factorials using $n! = n \\times (n-1)!$ until common factors appear.",
          "Cancel common factorial terms or factor out common factorial factors.",
          "Simplify the remaining arithmetic or algebraic expression."
        ],
